ent based on a specified path, ensuring the structure matches the path even if parts of it do not exist initially.\n2. **Input**: A document object representing the XML structure and a string representing the hierarchical path where the node should be created.\n3. **Output**: The newly created node at the specified path in the document.\n4. **Procedure**: \n   - The function first checks if the path points directly to the root. If so, it either returns the existing root or creates a new root element.\n   - If the path is deeper, it splits the path to identify the parent path and the target node.\n   - It then recursively ensures that the parent node exists by calling a related function.\n   - Depending on whether the target in the path is an attribute or an element, it either creates an attribute in the parent node or appends a new element to the parent.\n   - If the target node is an element and includes an index (though indexing is assumed to be 1 for simplicity), it still proceeds to create and append the element.\n   - The newly created node or attribute is then returned.\n","group":"karatelabs/karate","split":"dev","workload":"code-agent","kind":"function-description","gold":[{"path":"karate-core/src/main/java/com/intuit/karate/XmlUtils.java","grade":2,"spans":[[203,230]]}],"answerability":"answerable"},{"id":"repoqa-90c76657c3c38d570f8d","text":"\n1. **Purpose**: The function is designed to generate a JavaScript expression that accesses a specific element stored in a custom JavaScript object within the document's global scope.\n2. **Input**: A string representing a reference key to an element within a predefined JavaScript object.\n3. **Output**: A string that forms a JavaScript expression to access the element associated with the provided reference key.\n4. **Procedure**: The function concatenates the input reference key with a predefined path pointing to a custom JavaScript object in the document's global scope, forming a complete JavaScript expression that can be used to access the specified element.\n","group":"karatelabs/karate","split":"dev","workload":"code-agent","kind":"function-description","gold":[{"path":"karate-core/src/main/java/com/intuit/karate/driver/DriverOptions.java","grade":2,"spans":[[731,733]]}],"answerability":"answerable"},{"id":"repoqa-00ff38a5f5b2ed225f97","text":"\n1. **Purpose**: The method is designed to assign a specific scenario execution context to the current instance, enabling it to utilize this context in its operations.\n2. **Input**: The method accepts a single parameter that represents the scenario execution context.\n3. **Output**: There is no return value as the method's primary function is to set an internal state.\n4. **Procedure**: The method directly assigns the provided scenario execution context to an internal variable, effectively updating the state of the instance to reflect this new context.\n","group":"karatelabs/karate","split":"dev","workload":"code-agent","kind":"function-description","gold":[{"path":"karate-core/src/main/java/com/intuit/karate/http/WebSocketClient.java","grade":2,"spans":[[82,84]]}],"answerability":"answerable"},{"id":"repoqa-31c815486e93f3a63806","text":"\n1. **Purpose**: The function is designed to execute a command based on provided options and collect the output.\n2. **Input**: A map containing command execution parameters such as a single command line or a list of arguments.\n3. **Output**: A string representing the collected output from the executed command.\n4. **Procedure**: The function initiates a command execution process with the given parameters, waits for the command to complete, and then collects and returns the output from the command.\n","group":"karatelabs/karate","split":"dev","workload":"code-agent","kind":"function-description","gold":[{"path":"karate-core/src/main/java/com/intuit/karate/core/ScenarioBridge.java","grade":2,"spans":[[359,363]]}],"answerability":"answerable"},{"id":"repoqa-bcf191fe1868dbdb3fe4","text":"\n1. **Purpose**: The function is designed to execute a callable entity only once and cache the result for subsequent calls, ensuring efficiency and consistency in scenarios where the same expensive or deterministic operation might be repeatedly invoked.\n2. **Input**: The function takes a unique identifier for caching, a callable entity, an optional argument for the callable entity, and a flag indicating whether the scope should be shared across different calls.\n3. **Output**: Returns the result of the callable entity if it's not previously cached; otherwise, it returns the cached result. The output is wrapped in a custom object that can handle various data types.\n4. **Procedure**: \n   - Check if the result for the given unique identifier exists in the cache.\n   - If cached, retrieve and return the cached result, applying necessary scope adjustments.\n   - If not cached, acquire a lock to ensure that only one thread can execute the callable entity.\n   - Execute the callable entity with the provided argument.\n   - Cache the result along with any necessary configuration and variable states.\n   - Release the lock and return the newly cached result, applying scope adjustments as needed.\n","group":"karatelabs/karate","split":"dev","workload":"code-agent","kind":"function-description","gold":[{"path":"karate-core/src/main/java/com/intuit/karate/core/ScenarioEngine.java","grade":2,"spans":[[2000,2032]]}],"answerability":"answerable"},{"id":"repoqa-4f32e26064eeaf60edf9","text":"\n1. **Purpose**: The function is designed to fetch the current system time and format it into a human-readable string format.\n2. **Input**: It does not take any parameters.\n3. **Output**: Returns a string representing the current date and time, formatted according to a predefined pattern.\n4. **Procedure**: The function retrieves the current system date and time, then formats this timestamp using a predefined date format pattern to convert it into a string.\n","group":"karatelabs/karate","split":"dev","workload":"code-agent","kind":"function-description","gold":[{"path":"karate-core/src/main/java/com/intuit/karate/cli/IntellijHook.java","grade":2,"spans":[[91,93]]}],"answerability":"answerable"},{"id":"repoqa-b65af3f3d068fc47c5db","text":"\n1. **Purpose**: To determine if logging should be enabled for a specific web address.\n2. **Input**: A string representing the web address (URI).\n3. **Output**: A boolean value indicating whether logging is enabled for the given web address.\n4. **Procedure**: The method checks the provided web address against certain criteria (not detailed in the provided code snippet) to decide if logging activities should be performed for that specific URI.\n","group":"karatelabs/karate","split":"dev","workload":"code-agent","kind":"function-description","gold":[{"path":"karate-core/src/main/java/com/intuit/karate/http/HttpLogModifier.java","grade":2,"spans":[[32,32]]}],"answerability":"answerable"},{"id":"repoqa-3286ce3b412727b25e0c","text":"\n1. **Purpose**: The function is designed to generate a JSON string that specifies the method and value for locating a UI element in a Windows application based on different types of identifiers.\n2. **Input**: A string identifier that may start with specific prefixes to denote the type of locator (e.g., XPath, accessibility ID, ID, or name).\n3. **Output**: A JSON string that describes the locator type and the value to be used for finding the element.\n4. **Procedure**: The function checks the prefix of the input string to determine the locator type (XPath, accessibility ID, ID, or name). It then constructs a JSON object with two properties: \"using\" (the locator type) and \"value\" (the actual identifier, with any prefix removed if necessary). This JSON object is then converted to a string and returned.\n","group":"karatelabs/karate","split":"dev","workload":"code-agent","kind":"function-description","gold":[{"path":"karate-core/src/main/java/com/intuit/karate/driver/microsoft/WinAppDriver.java","grade":2,"spans":[[58,70]]}],"answerability":"answerable"},{"id":"repoqa-5446db087618222aa1d3","text":"\n1. **Purpose**: To enhance the template processing capabilities by incorporating additional template resolution strategies into the engine.\n2. **Input**: An instance of a template resolver interface, which defines how templates should be located and resolved.\n3. **Output**: There is no direct output; the method modifies the internal state of the template engine by registering the new resolver.\n4. **Procedure**: The method takes the provided template resolver and adds it to the template engine's list of resolvers. This allows the engine to use the new resolver along with any previously added resolvers to locate and process templates based on various criteria specified within each resolver.\n","group":"karatelabs/karate","split":"dev","workload":"code-agent","kind":"function-description","gold":[{"path":"karate-core/src/main/java/com/intuit/karate/template/KarateTemplateEngine.java","grade":2,"spans":[[74,76]]}],"answerability":"answerable"}]}
